        "original_id": "1acd44d5-b184-556c-a0a1-647f00baf984",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Istanbul, Turkey, Ayakapı, Yenisahra, Sultanahmet Meydanı\n- Buildings and Sites: Gül Mosque, Hippodrome of Constantinople\n- Organizations/Groups: Ottomans\n\nFacts:\n- Gül Mosque is a former Eastern Orthodox church in Istanbul, Turkey.\n- Gül Mosque was converted into a mosque by the Ottomans.\n- Ayakapı contains the Gül Mosque, described as one of the most important surviving Byzantine buildings on Istanbul's historical peninsula.\n- The Hippodrome of Constantinople is today a square in Istanbul named Sultanahmet Meydanı.\n- A few fragments of the original Hippodrome of Constantinople structure survive.\n- Yenisahra is a neighbourhood in the Ataşehir district of Istanbul, Turkey.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "e8b665e6-b461-5c79-9678-6f9c75d8643c",
        "text": "Page: Gül Mosque. Sentence: Gül Mosque (Turkish: \"Gül Camii\" , meaning: \"The Mosque of the Rose\" in English) is a former Eastern Orthodox church in Istanbul, Turkey, converted into a mosque by the Ottomans.\nPage: Hippodrome of Constantinople. Sentence: Today it is a square named \"Sultanahmet Meydanı\" (\"Sultan Ahmet Square\") in the Turkish city of Istanbul, with a few fragments of the original structure surviving.\nPage: Ayakapı. Sentence: In Ayakapı lies one of the most important surviving Byzantine buildings of the historical peninsula, the Gül Mosque.\nPage: Yenisahra. Sentence: Yenisahra is a neighbourhood of the Ataşehir district in Istanbul, Turkey.",
